<!-- Begin Title -->
<h1>Product</h1>
<!-- End Title -->
<hr class="margin-t-1-8 margin-b-3-4">
<!-- Begin Total Content -->
<!-- Begin Content -->
<div class="entry-content">
<p>
The term &quot;product&quot; refers to the result of one or more <a href="/Multiplication.html">multiplications</a>. For example, the mathematical statement <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline1.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="65"
 height="20" alt="a&#215;b=c" /> would be read &quot;<img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline2.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="a" /> <a href="/Times.html">times</a> <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline3.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="b" /> <a href="/Equal.html">equals</a> <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline4.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="8"
 height="20" alt="c" />,&quot; where <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline5.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="a" /> is called the <a href="/Multiplier.html">multiplier</a>, <img
 src="/images/equations/Product/Inline6.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%"
 border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="b" /> the <a href="/Multiplicand.html">multiplicand</a>
 and <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline7.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%"
 border="0" width="8" height="20" alt="c" /> is their product. In addition, <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline8.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="a" /> and <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline9.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="b" />
 are <a href="/Factor.html">factors</a> of <img src="/images/equations/Product/Inline10.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="8"
 height="20" alt="c" />.
</p>
<p>
More generally, it is possible to take the product of many different kinds of mathematical objects, including those that are not numbers. For example, the product of two sets
 is given by the <a href="/CartesianProduct.html">Cartesian product</a>. In <a href="/Topology.html">topology</a>,
 the product of <a href="/Space.html">spaces</a> can be defined by using the <a href="/ProductTopology.html">product
 topology</a>. The product of two <a href="/Group.html">groups</a>, <a href="/VectorSpace.html">vector
 spaces</a>, or <a href="/Module.html">modules</a> is given by the <a href="/DirectProduct.html">direct
 product</a>. In <a href="/CategoryTheory.html">category theory</a>, the product of
 <a href="/Object.html">objects</a> is given using the <a href="/CategoryProduct.html">category
 product</a>.
</p>
<p>
The product symbol is defined by
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/Product/NumberedEquation1.svg" class="numberedequation" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="131" height="48" alt=" product_(i=1)^nf_i=f_1&#183;f_2...f_n. " /></td><td
 align="right" width="3">
<div id="eqn1" class="eqnum">
(1)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
Useful product identities include
</p>
